  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThere are two main talking points for the forecast period. The first \nis the continued heat across the region, driven by a strong upper \nridge located overhead. And the second is the chances for severe \nstorms on Saturday as the ridge shifts westward...briefly.\n\nFirst of all, the upper ridge responsible for our heatwave this week \nwill remain situated over the southern Appalachian region today and \nFriday. Deterministic guidance shows highs in the 95-100 degree \nrange throughout the valley this afternoon and again on Friday. \nDewpoints will remain in the upper 60s to low 70s both days, \nresulting in afternoon heat index values in the 100-105 degree \nrange. Model guidance shows the ridge being almost perfectly \ncentered over the MRX forecast area during the day tomorrow, with \nan expansion in warmer H85 temps noted. I could see us eking out \nanother degree or two above what the NBM guidance is showing, \nwhich is also supported by some guidance sources including the \nECMWF. If that happens, advisory criteria would definitely be \nreached further north. Since we're going to be close to advisory\ncriteria today in areas outside the Heat Advisory that was issued\nyesterday, and there's a decent shot we'd need it tomorrow, I went\nahead and expanded it effective this afternoon. The ridge shifts \nwest on Saturday, allowing a \"cold\" front to move through, and \nremains centered over the Great Plains through Tuesday before \nshifting back east again. In other words, the heat returns next \nweek it seems. Lastly, I've included a Climate section below to \nhighlight some potential records that are in jeopardy in the \ncoming days.\n\nWith regards to severe chances on Saturday, a deep trough digs over \nthe west coast Friday into Saturday, allowing the ridge to shift \nwest to the plains and a trough to strengthen along the eastern \nseaboard. This will send at least one impulse through the Ohio \nvalley, southern Appalachians, and into the Carolinas on Saturday \nand push a cold front through the region. Forecast soundings show \nsome rather extreme instability values over the forecast area on \nSaturday afternoon, characterized by mixed layer CAPE values of \n2,500 to 4,000 J/kg, coincident with mid level lapse rates of over \n6.5 C/km in some cases. Any storm that can get a sustained updraft \ngoing in that environment will have little to no issues become \nstrong to severe. Downbursts and even large hail would be on the \ntable, despite freezing levels above 13,000 ft. Thankfully, shear is \non the low-end of the spectrum in terms of supporting organized \nconvection, so coverage is expected to be somewhat sparse.\n\nThe \"cold\" front knocks highs down into the 80s to 90s for Sunday \nthrough Tuesday, but as I mentioned above the ridge shifts back east \nby mid-week next week so I suspect we'll see a return of quite warm \ntemperatures. \n\n---- Climate section ----\n\nI wanted to highlight the Chattanooga area in the climate section. \nCurrently they're sitting at 30 straight days (valid 9/2/2026) of \nhighs at/above 90 degrees, which is tied for 4th place. High \ntemperatures are forecast to remain at/above 90 degrees through the \nforecast period. Unless something goes off the rails and a \nthunderstorm impacts their max temps one day, there's a very good \nchance they break the all time record of consecutive days at/above \n90 next week. Contrast this with Tri-Cities and Knoxville, which \ndon't have active streaks that even rank in the top 50.\n\nBelow are some tables that highlight daily record highs for all 4 \nclimate sites through next week, as well as some information on the \n90 degree day streak for Chattanooga specifically.\n\nChattanooga 90 Degree Day Streak\n\nRank  Days   Ending Date\n1     38     08-02-1993\n2     32     08-29-2007\n3     31     07-21-1954 \n4     30     09-02-2026 \n5     29     08-16-2010\n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge      \n09-03   100(1954)      98(2011)       96(2011)       99(2011)       \n09-04   102(1954)      101(1954)      99(1954)       100(1954)      \n09-05   104(1925)      103(1954)      99(1954)       102(1954)      \n09-06   103(1925)      101(1954)      100(1954)      102(1954)      \n09-07   104(1925)      102(1925)      100(1954)      99(1954)       \n09-08   100(1925)      96(1941)       92(2002)       95(1999)       \n09-09   102(1925)      100(1925)      95(1954)       97(1954)",
